WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.080
There was very interesting breaking news&nbsp;
last night that the Senate majority leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.920
Mitch McConnell, who of course is not just a&nbsp;
Republican but has been one of the most pernicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:17.280
Republicans to the causes of sanity and reason for&nbsp;
a long time and has been very effective at getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.200
endless numbers of right wing judges confirmed&nbsp;
successfully obstructed Barack Obama's Supreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:30.720
Court nomination of Merrick Garland back in&nbsp;
2016, that Mitch McConnell is reportedly pleased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:37.360
about Donald Trump being impeached a second time.
Why? Because he believes it will make it easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:44.000
for the Republican Party to purge Donald&nbsp;
Trump to wash its hands of an association&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.040
with Donald Trump. Mitch McConnell has&nbsp;
told associates this has been reported by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.680
The New York Times and confirmed by Fox News.
So take take your pick. At this point, Mitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.960
McConnell has told associates that he does believe&nbsp;
Donald Trump committed impeachable offenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:06.480
and that he is pleased that Democrats are&nbsp;
going forward with a second impeachment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.160
the president. He believes it will make it&nbsp;
easier to purge Trump from the party. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.080
at the same time, Representative Kevin&nbsp;
McCarthy of California, the minority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:25.120
leader and a big supporter of Donald Trump,&nbsp;
has reportedly been asking other Republicans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:33.120
should I call on Donald Trump to resign? And&nbsp;
McCarthy has been personally against impeachment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.520
And he said he and other party leaders have&nbsp;
decided not to lobby Republicans to vote no. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.640
an aide to McCarthy said he's open to a measure&nbsp;
censuring Donald Trump in private. McCarthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:52.160
reached out to a leading House Democrat to see if&nbsp;
the chamber would be willing to look at a censure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:59.120
vote instead of impeachment. Nancy Pelosi has&nbsp;
since ruled that out. So this is these truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.720
are unique times in American political history.&nbsp;
And when you take this together, McConnell is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.880
pleased that impeachment is moving forward.&nbsp;
And McCarthy has been calling around saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.320
what about censure? We're not going to lobby&nbsp;
for no votes on impeachment. What are we doing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:20.640
This reflects, depending on your perspective,&nbsp;
a come to Jesus moment for the Republican Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.080
or political posturing and opportunism.&nbsp;
Now, maybe you think I'm cynical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.400
but I think the answer is obvious. This&nbsp;
is political opportunism. And in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:38.320
I don't believe that McConnell is having a change&nbsp;
of heart on on Donald Trump. I believe McConnell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.680
has had the same view on Trump all along. And&nbsp;
he said for as long as I can get judges through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.480
and block things Democrats want to do will&nbsp;
sort of stand aside as Donald Trump does.&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.240
What he does here, by the way, is&nbsp;
the report I read to you is from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.720
The New York Times, Laura Ingraham on Fox&nbsp;
News also saying they've confirmed McConnell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.640
is not going to stand in the way of impeachment&nbsp;
and is, quote, done with Donald Trump or their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.520
FOX News has confirmed that while he's not said&nbsp;
whether he supports conviction in the Senate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.960
Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell is not&nbsp;
going to stand in the way of this impeachment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:18.000
effort and is said to be done with Trump. Now,&nbsp;
given all that, and that ultimately is the key.&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.480
Does Mitch McConnell support conviction of Donald&nbsp;
Trump on impeachment? Will he vote to convict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:31.280
when the Senate takes up this second impeachment&nbsp;
of Donald Trump with just minutes ago, as we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:38.800
been reporting here, was walked over by the House&nbsp;
impeachment managers to the United States Senate?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.720
I don't know the answer to that. My instinct is&nbsp;
probably not. At the same time, Mitch McConnell's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.200
wife, Elaine Chao, was one of the first to resign&nbsp;
in last week's run of resignations. She was Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.480
Trump's transportation secretary. I believe&nbsp;
that this is nothing other than Mitch McConnell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.520
doing what he likely planned to do for a long&nbsp;
time. Mitch McConnell has probably hated Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.440
for a while. But like most Republicans, he was&nbsp;
either afraid of Trump's cult or calculating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.240
that it didn't make sense to come out against&nbsp;
Trump to the extent that he could keep getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:17.440
judges confirmed and get tax reform done and on&nbsp;
and on and on. And so I don't think McConnell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:25.840
deserves any any any praise for this.&nbsp;
What he wants is for Nancy Pelosi's name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:32.960
and Democrats to be attached in public&nbsp;
to this impeachment. And he wants to hide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.600
and figure out how he can use this to his&nbsp;
advantage to get this mess cleaned up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.800
the mess being the destruction that Donald Trump&nbsp;
has brought to the Republican Party. So there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.880
no praise here for Mitch McConnell. Complicity&nbsp;
due to being afraid is still complicity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.040
complicity or indifference due to political&nbsp;
opportunism or self aggrandizement or person. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:01.840
benefit, it's all just as bad Mitch McConnell got&nbsp;
his judges and now he wants to get rid of his old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:08.160
baggage and it may work. Unfortunately, there is a&nbsp;
very short political memory in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.680
We've already forgotten about things that happened&nbsp;
six months ago that appeared to be really big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.560
deals at this point. And so the big narratives are&nbsp;
what matter. And the big narrative Mitch McConnell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:25.280
wants is I didn't stand in the way of impeachment.&nbsp;
I used that second impeachment to try to wash our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:31.520
hands of Trump. I got my judges done. We got our&nbsp;
Supreme Court justices and now let's get rid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.880
the baggage. None of them deserve to have their&nbsp;
reputations rehabilitated. I've said that a lot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.400
And that is all that this ultimately is. And&nbsp;
where I get to in the end is what I mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.720
before. Will Mitch McConnell vote to convict&nbsp;
in the impeachment trial of Donald Trump in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.840
Senate? I don't know the answer. Maybe tomorrow&nbsp;
I'll have some stronger feelings about it.

